vulkan_async_submission (off, requires restart): EndSubmission hands the recorded deferred command stream, the
submission's command buffer, fence and wait semaphores to a "Vulkan Submission" thread, which replays the stream into
the command buffer and submits it, so the driver's command recording leaves the GPU command processor thread.
vulkan_async_submission_draws (2000, hot reload) also ends the submission every this many draws outside occlusion
queries. A fence is polled or waited for only once the thread has passed it to vkQueueSubmit (Vulkan requires a fence
being submitted to be externally synchronized); a blocking wait and a swap first wait for the thread to empty its
queue. A failed submission is reported as a lost device.
With gpu_frame_stats on, every 10 s the log gives the CPU time per frame of the command processor and submission
threads (thread CPU time, waits excluded) next to the frame time.

Off by default: measured standing in the city (Release, RTX 5070 Ti, ~4,600 draws a frame), the command processor
thread takes ~8.0 ms of a ~8.2 ms frame without it (~122 FPS, as D3D12 at the same spot); with it, ~7.2 ms plus
~1.4 ms on the submission thread, but the frame takes ~8.8 ms (~113 FPS): the swap waits for the last chunk's replay
and the extra submissions break render passes. Replaying the stream is only ~1 ms of the command processor's frame.

